.\" The macros used below are those described in groff_man(7) and
 \" groff_man_style(7).
.
.TH guild 1 "2025-12-12" "@GUILE_VERSION@" "GNU Guile"
.
.SH NAME
guild, guile-tools \- run Guile-related commands
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY guild
.B compile
.YS
.SY guild
.B disassemble
.YS
.SY guild
.B display\-commentary
.YS
.SY guild
.B doc\-snarf
.YS
.SY guild
.B help
.YS
.SY guild
.B use2dot
.YS
.SY .\|.\|.
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run Guile-related commands; \(dqguild help --all\(dq will provide a
complete list.
.B guile\-tools
is an older, deprecated name for this program.
.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B help \fR[\fB--all \fR| \fICOMMAND]
Display a list of
.B \-\-all
available subcommands, information about
.IR COMMAND ,
or summary information.
.TP
.B compile \fR[\fIARG \fR\&.\|.\|.\&]
Invoke the Guile compiler.
.TP
.B disassemble \fR[\fIFILE \fR\&.\|.\|.\&]
Disassemble a compiled .go file.
.TP
.B display\-commentary \fR[\fIREF \fR\&.\|.\|.\&]
Display Commentary section from
.IR REF1 , \ REF2
and so on.  Each
.I REF
may be a filename or module name (list of symbols).  In the latter case,
a filename is computed by searching
.BR %load-path.
.TP
.BI doc\-snarf \ FILE
Read a Scheme source file to extract docstrings in the format specified
in
.B DOCSTRINGS
below, and infer a procedure prototype from procedure definition lines
starting with
.BR "(define... )" .
.
Currently, two output modes are implemented: texinfo and plaintext.
Default is plaintext, texinfo can be switched on with the
.B \-\-texinfo
or
.B \-t
command line option.
.TP
.BI use2dot \ \fR[\fB\-m \ \fIMODULE] \ [\fIFILE \fR\&.\|.\|.\&]
Display to stdout a graphviz DOT specification that describes module
dependencies in the
.IR FILE s.

A top-level
.B use-modules
form or a
.BR :use-module
component in a
.B define-module
form results in a \(dqsolid\(dq style edge.

An
.B :autoload
component in a
.B define-module
form results in a \(dqdotted\(dq style edge with label \(dqN\(dq
indicating that N names are responsible for triggering the autoload.
(The \(dqN\(dq label is not implemented.)

A top-level
.B load or
.B primitive-load form results in a a \(dqbold\(dq style
edge to a node named with either the file name if the
.B load
argument is a string, or \(dq[computed in \fIFILE\fR]\(dq otherwise.

The
.BR \-\-default-module
.I MODULE
(or
.BR \-m)
option sets
.I MODULE
as the default for top-level
.B use-modules
forms that do not follow a
.B define-module
form in a file.
.I MODULE
should be be a list, or
.BR #f ,
in which case such top-level
.B use-modules forms are effectively
ignored.  The default value is
.B (guile-user).
.
.SH DOCSTRINGS
A docstring can span multiple lines and begins with a line that starts
with \(dq;;\(dq (two semicolons and a space). A docstring ends with a
line that begins with \(dq(define ...)\(dq or with one or more
\(dqoption\(dq lines that begin with \(dq;;-\(dq (two semicolons and a
dash), which is then followed by a keyword, a colon, and a string.

Additionally, \(dqstandard internal docstrings\(dq (for Scheme source)
are recognized and output as \(dqoptions\(dq.  The output formatting is
likely to change in the future.

Here's an example:

.RS 4
.EX
;; This procedure foos, or bars, depending on the argument @var{braz}.
;;-Author: Martin Grabmueller
(define (foo/bar braz)
  (if braz 'foo 'bar))
.EE
.RE

which produces this when the output format is texinfo:

.RS 4
.EX
#!
.
foo/bar
@deffn procedure foo/bar braz
This procedure foos, or bars, depending on the argument @var{braz}.
@c Author: Martin Grabmueller
@end deffn
!#

.EE
.RE

or this when the output format is plain text:

.RS 4
.EX
#!
Procedure: foo/bar braz
This procedure foos, or bars, depending on the argument @var{braz}.
 Author: Martin Grabmueller
^L
!#
.EE
.RE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.
.IR dot (1),
.IR guile (1),
and for much more detail, the
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/manual/html_node/index.html
Guile Reference Manual
.UE
which may also be available via
.BR info\ guile
or
.B M\-x info
in emacs.
